module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Reflexive.ExtensionChain.StalkCriterion
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSequence.KoszulExtStalk
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Modules.QuotientRingSheafEpi

/-!
# The structure sheaf of the Koszul-regular quotient, and its stalks

Let `O♯` be a sheaf of commutative rings on a space and let `φ : (O♯)ʳ ⟶ O♯` be a morphism of
sheaves of modules. The quotient of `O♯` by the sheaf of ideals that `φ` generates is a sheaf of
**commutative rings** `O_X`, and the cokernel of `φ` — the quotient read in sheaves of
`O♯`-modules — is its underlying module. This file names those objects and their stalks, and
records the two structures that the stalk carries:

* the stalk `(O_X)_x` is a commutative ring, being the stalk of a sheaf of commutative rings;
* it is an algebra over the stalk `O_x`, along the stalk of the quotient map.

and identifies the stalk of the cokernel of `φ` with it.

The point of naming these is that `(O_X)_x` is the ring over which the comparison of Stacks 0BQY
is to be read at a stalk. It is *not* necessary to present it as a quotient `O_x ⧸ J_x` of the
stalk of `O♯`: the base-change identification of
`StacksFrontier/LinearAlgebra/ExteriorPower/HomBaseChange.lean` is stated for an arbitrary
commutative algebra over the base ring, and the stalk of a sheaf exterior power over `O_X` is
natively an exterior power over `(O_X)_x`. So both sides of the comparison already speak
`(O_X)_x`, and a presentation of it as a quotient would be a detour through an object neither
side needs.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.RegularSequence.quotientIdeal`: the sheaf of ideals generated by `φ`.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.quotientStructureSheaf`: the quotient `O_X`, as a sheaf of commutative
  rings.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.quotientStructureStalk`: its stalk at a point, a commutative ring.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.quotientStalkRingHom`: the stalk of the quotient map, a ring
  homomorphism `O_x ⟶ (O_X)_x`.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.quotientStalkAlgebra`: the resulting algebra structure.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.cokernelStalkEquivStructureStalk`: the stalk of the cokernel of `φ` is
  `(O_X)_x`, as an equivalence of types.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.qUnitModule` and
  `Stacks.RegularSequence.unitStalkBaseLinearEquiv`: the unit module of `O_X` with scalars
  restricted, and the identification of its stalk with `(O_X)_x` **linearly over the stalk of
  `O♯`**.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.cokernelStalkLinearEquivStructureStalk`: the same identification as the
  previous item in the list, linearly over the stalk of `O♯` — the form that transports a module
  of homomorphisms.

## Implementation notes

**The universes of the quotient constructions must be pinned by hand.** `Stacks.Sites`'
quotient-ring-sheaf constructions take the site, its topology and the structure sheaf as implicit
arguments, and the sheaf of ideals is an abbreviation for a submodule, from which the site cannot
be recovered by unification. Applied to a sheaf on a space the universes therefore default to
zero and the application fails with a mismatch between `Type 1` and `Type (u + 1)`, which reads
as a spelling problem and is not one. Writing the universes explicitly — and supplying the
structure sheaf by name where the morphism is phrased over `CategoryTheory.Sheaf.toRingCat`,
since recovering it from that spelling would mean inverting `sheafCompose` — is what makes these
elaborate. That is the whole of the difficulty here.

**The algebra structure is a definition and not an instance.** It is introduced where needed with
`letI`, which is this library's idiom for scalar structures on stalks; making it global would put
an algebra instance on every stalk of every sheaf of this shape.

**The stalk is an abbreviation, deliberately.** `quotientStructureStalk` is reducible so that the
algebra structure below is found by instance search at the unfolded spelling as well as at the
named one. With it a definition, a goal mentioning `TopCat.Presheaf.stalk (O_X).obj x` directly
fails to resolve `HSMul` against an algebra stated at the named form. The quotient *sheaf* above
is left a definition, since what must stay opaque there is the sheafification.

**Four presentations of the ring presheaf, and only one reducible.** The identification of the
stalk of the cokernel with the stalk of `O_X` is proved on germs, and a germ-level proof here
crosses between the module-stalk API and the ring-stalk API. Those are written against *four*
different presentations of the ring presheaf underlying a sheaf of commutative rings `Rc`:

* `Rc.obj`, the `CommRingCat`-valued presheaf itself;
* `CategoryTheory.Sheaf.toRingCat Rc`, **the only reducible one** — it is by construction the
  syntactic composite `Rc.obj ⋙ forget₂ CommRingCat RingCat`, and its docstring says it exists
  for exactly this reason;
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf Rc`, which goes through
  `sheafCompose` and is *not* reducible;
* `sheafToPresheaf` applied to `Rc`, which appears in the statements of the `PresheafOfModules`
  stalk lemmas.

A mismatch between any two of them presents as a failed rewrite or an unresolved instance and
reads as a spelling problem, which it is — but not one the elaborator will close on its own.
Three consequences were load-bearing here. The restricted unit module is spelled over `toRingCat`
so that the stalk identification of a unit module applies to it at all. The crossing between the
`CommRingCat` and `RingCat` stalks of the scalars is **already solved in the library**, by
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.SheafHomStalk.germAt_smul` and its companion `germAt_smul_ring`, whose
surrounding note records that the two `Module` instances are literally the same term; reaching
instead for `PresheafOfModules.germ_smul` meets the `sheafToPresheaf` spelling and fails. And
where the restricted action has to be recognised as the action of the image section, that holds
definitionally but cannot be *written* — the restricted module carries only the action of `Rc` —
so the step is taken by applying a term, where definitional unfolding is available, rather than
by rewriting.

This is the third kind of object on which the multiplicity of spellings has been load-bearing in
this development, after the four same-named `sectionRestrict_smul` lemmas with different argument
orders and the `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf`-against-`toRingCat` mismatch in the
quotient constructions. It is a property of the library, not an accident of this file.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BQY](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BQY)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CategoryTheory.Sheaf Opposite TopologicalSpace
open AlgebraicGeometry

universe u

namespace Stacks.RegularSequence

open Stacks.Sites Stacks.SheafOfModules Stacks.SheafOfModules.SheafHomStalk
open Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ReflexiveModules

variable {X : TopCat.{u}} (Rc : TopCat.Sheaf CommRingCat.{u} X)
  [HasSheafify (Opens.grothendieckTopology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
  [(Opens.grothendieckTopology X).WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
  [HasWeakSheafify (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥X) CommRingCat.{u}]
  {r : ℕ}
  (φ : koszulFreeSheaf Rc r ⟶
    SheafOfModules.unit (toRingCat (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥X) Rc))

/-- **The sheaf of ideals generated by `φ`.** The structure sheaf is supplied by name because the
morphism is phrased over `CategoryTheory.Sheaf.toRingCat`, from which it cannot be recovered by
unification. Reducible, so that the local-surjectivity instance for the quotient map is found
through it. -/
abbrev quotientIdeal := idealOfHom (O := Rc) (F := koszulFreeSheaf Rc r) φ

/-- **The quotient `O_X` of `O♯` by the sheaf of ideals generated by `φ`, as a sheaf of
commutative rings.** -/
def quotientStructureSheaf := quotientRingSheaf.{u, u, u} (quotientIdeal Rc φ)

/-- **The stalk of `O_X` at a point**, a commutative ring. This is the ring over which the
comparison of Stacks 0BQY is read at a stalk. -/
abbrev quotientStructureStalk (x : X) :=
  TopCat.Presheaf.stalk (quotientStructureSheaf Rc φ).obj x

/-- **The stalk of the quotient map**, a ring homomorphism `O_x ⟶ (O_X)_x`. -/
def quotientStalkRingHom (x : X) :=
  (TopCat.Presheaf.stalkFunctor CommRingCat.{u} x).map
    (toQuotientRingSheaf.{u, u, u} (quotientIdeal Rc φ)).hom

/-- **The stalk of `O_X` is an algebra over the stalk of `O♯`**, along the stalk of the quotient
map. This is a definition rather than an instance; introduce it with `letI` where it is needed. -/
@[instance_reducible]
def quotientStalkAlgebra (x : X) :
    Algebra ↑(ringStalk Rc x) ↑(quotientStructureStalk Rc φ x) :=
  RingHom.toAlgebra (quotientStalkRingHom Rc φ x).hom

/-- The unit module of `O_X` with its scalars restricted along the quotient map. Its sections are
those of `O_X`; the ring sheaf is spelled with `CategoryTheory.Sheaf.toRingCat`, which is
reducible, so that the stalk identification of a unit module applies to it. -/
def qUnitModule :=
  (SheafOfModules.restrictScalars (quotientRingHom (quotientIdeal Rc φ))).obj
    (SheafOfModules.unit
      (toRingCat (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥X) (quotientStructureSheaf Rc φ)))

/-- **The stalk of `O_X` is the stalk of its unit module, linearly over the stalk of `O♯`.** The
two actions of `O♯` agree: on the left it acts through the quotient map by restriction of
scalars, on the right through the same map by the algebra structure, and the identification is
the identity on underlying elements.

Proved on germs. A germ of `O♯` and a germ of the module are brought onto a common open, the
scalar is moved inside the germ by `germAt_smul`, the restricted action is recognised as the
action of the image section — which holds definitionally, so it is applied as a term rather than
rewritten — and the germ of that image is the image of the germ by
`TopCat.Presheaf.stalkFunctor_map_germ_apply`. -/
def unitStalkBaseLinearEquiv (x : X) :=
  letI := quotientStalkAlgebra Rc φ x
  ({ unitModuleStalkAddEquiv (quotientStructureSheaf Rc φ).obj x with
      map_smul' := fun c m => by
        obtain ⟨U, hyU, a, rfl⟩ := TopCat.Presheaf.exists_germ_eq (X := X) Rc.obj c
        obtain ⟨V, hyV, b, rfl⟩ := exists_germ_eq (qUnitModule Rc φ) m
        have hyUV : x ∈ U ⊓ V := ⟨hyU, hyV⟩
        rw [← TopCat.Presheaf.germ_res_apply (X := X) Rc.obj
            (homOfLE (inf_le_left : U ⊓ V ≤ U)) x hyUV a,
          ← germAt_resLE (qUnitModule Rc φ) (inf_le_right : U ⊓ V ≤ V) hyUV b,
          ← germAt_smul (qUnitModule Rc φ) hyUV _ _]
        refine (unitModuleStalkAddEquiv_germ_smul (quotientStructureSheaf Rc φ).obj x (U ⊓ V)
            hyUV ((quotientRingHom (quotientIdeal Rc φ)).hom.app (op (U ⊓ V))
              ((Rc.obj.map (homOfLE (inf_le_left : U ⊓ V ≤ U)).op) a))
            (resLE (qUnitModule Rc φ) (inf_le_right : U ⊓ V ≤ V) b)).trans ?_
        rw [smul_eq_mul, Algebra.smul_def]
        congr 1
        exact (TopCat.Presheaf.stalkFunctor_map_germ_apply (U ⊓ V) x hyUV
          (toQuotientRingSheaf.{u, u, u} (quotientIdeal Rc φ)).hom _).symm } :
    modStalk (Rc := Rc) (qUnitModule Rc φ) x ≃ₗ[ringStalk Rc x]
      ↑(quotientStructureStalk Rc φ x))

/-- The stalk of the cokernel of `φ`, compared with the stalk of the underlying module of `O_X`.
This is the stalk of the identification of the cokernel with the quotient ring sheaf. -/
def cokernelStalkEquivUnitStalk (x : X) :=
  modStalkEquivOfIso Rc
    (cokernelIsoQuotientRingOfHom (O := Rc) (F := koszulFreeSheaf Rc r) φ) x

/-- **The stalk of the cokernel of `φ` is the stalk of `O_X`.** The cokernel of `φ` is the
underlying module of `O_X`, and the stalk of the underlying module of a sheaf of rings is the
stalk of that sheaf of rings.

This is an equivalence of types: its two factors are linear over different rings, the first over
`O_x` and the second over `(O_X)_x`. -/
def cokernelStalkEquivStructureStalk (x : X) :=
  (cokernelStalkEquivUnitStalk Rc φ x).toEquiv.trans
    (unitModuleStalkLinearEquiv (quotientStructureSheaf Rc φ).obj x).toEquiv

/-- **The stalk of the cokernel of `φ` is the stalk of `O_X`, linearly over the stalk of `O♯`.**
The composite of the stalk of the identification of the cokernel with the quotient ring sheaf and
the linear identification of the stalk of a unit module.

This is the form the comparison of Stacks 0BQY consumes: being linear over the stalk of `O♯`, it
transports a module of `O♯`-linear homomorphisms, which an equivalence of types would not. -/
def cokernelStalkLinearEquivStructureStalk (x : X) :=
  letI := quotientStalkAlgebra Rc φ x
  (cokernelStalkEquivUnitStalk Rc φ x) ≪≫ₗ (unitStalkBaseLinearEquiv Rc φ x)

end Stacks.RegularSequence

end
